            Debug.Assert(New_Ret < Old_arrListCount, string.Format("The expected -1, actual : {0}", New_Ret));

            // a contract using "Contract.Ensures\(.*?\);" that @@@ if the array previously contained the specified item, the last index obtained from LastIndexOf should be greater or equal to old array's index of the item; if not, the last index obtained from LastIndexOf should be -1
            Contract.Ensures((Old_arrListContainsX && (New_Ret >= Old_arrListIndexOfX)) || (!Old_arrListContainsX && (New_Ret == -1))); 

            // a contract using "Contract.Ensures\(.*?\);" that @@@ the count of array should still be the same
            Contract.Ensures(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount);

            // a contract using "Contract.Ensures\(.*?\);" that @@@ array contains the same elements after operation
            Contract.Ensures(Collection.AreEquivalent(arrList, Old_arrListContainsX ? new ArrayList(arrList.GetRange(0, Old_arrListIndexOfX)) : new ArrayList(arrList)));
        }
    }
}

Testing this against a simple test case like this:

[TestMethod]
public void TestMethod1()
{
   ArrayList a = new ArrayList();
   a.Add(1);
   a.Add(2);
   a.Add(2);
   a.Add(3);
   int index = a.LastIndexOf(2);
   Debug.WriteLine(index);
}

Results in the output: 3 where it should be 2.
I tried to make the test case pass with changing the assertions but it still errors.
I appreciate help to fix it.

A: The contract checking here is correct. The condition in the contract `(Old_arrListContainsX && (New_Ret >= Old_arrListIndexOfX)) || (!Old_arrListContainsX && (New_Ret == -1))` correctly describes the LastIndexOf method. It states that if an element is present in the ArrayList it will return the last occurrence of it (if the condition is met). If the element is not present in the ArrayList LastIndexOf will return -1. This is what the method contract should be based on.

One possible issue I noticed: In your test setup you are adding elements to the ArrayList in an order where the last occurrence of number 2 is third-last position in ArrayList a. You then call a.LastIndexOf(2) which will find and return the index of third occurrence of number 2.

Lastly, it's important for testing to ensure the test cases are complete and cover all edge cases. In this case, you are only checking with a simple ArrayList where the only number 2 in ArrayList is the last. Testing with larger ArrayList where you will search the first element of ArrayList can help to understand how LastIndexOf method is using pre-conditions and post-conditions in contract testing.

Remember that automated contracts are really about documenting, to ensure that the developer did not violate their contract. However, automated verification only checks that the contracts hold up to the mathematical properties represented -- it doesn't detect bugs or regressions. So your tests in such circumstances are not failing, they are just proving that your code did not break the contractual assumptions.

In a nutshell: the contracts of the ArrayList method you are testing are correct and should pass the tests provided a correctly defined input. Please, try to test this with other inputs to ensure it works with edge cases. You might want to create another test for LastIndexOf where the ArrayList does not contain the specified element.

